s a center section of the target called the aiming mark, which was 20 cm in diameter, and hits on the aiming mark was the first tie-breaker."] "\n\n" [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/43646"} "Edwin Vásquez"] " won the event to win Peru’s first gold medal ever, scoring 545. Of his six series, his lowest score was 89, easily the most consistent shooter. There was a three-way tie for second place at 539, between " [:a {:href "/athletes/44097"} "Rudolf Schnyder"] " of Switzerland, " [:a {:href "/athletes/44294"} "Torsten Ullman"] " of Sweden, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/44615"} "Joe Benner"] " of the United States. Both Schnyder and Ullman had all shots in the aiming mark, while Benner had only 58 and was placed 4th. The second tie-breaker was greatest number of number of 10s, and Schnyder won the silver medal on this, scoring 21 10s against Ullman’s 16. This was Ullman’s third shooting medal, as he had won " [:a {:href "/results/51635"} "gold"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/51634"} "bronze"] " in the pistol events in 1936. Rudolf Schnyder was the son of " [:a {:href "/athletes/44098"} "Willy Schnyder"] ", a former UIT World Champion in free pistol."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {} "Round 1 Points"] [:th {} "Round 2 Points"] [:th {} "Round 3 Points"] [:th {} "Round 4 Points"] [:th {} "Round 5 Points"] [:th {} "Round 6 Points"] [:th {} "Hits"] [:th {} "Xs"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/43646"} "Edwin Vásquez"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/PER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/PER.png"}] "PER"]] [:td {} "545"] [:td {} "93"] [:td {} "89"] [:td {} "89"] [:td {} "91"] [:td {} "90"] [:td {} "93"] [:td {} "60"] [:td {} "21"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :st